  "text": "Allergies:\nGantrisin Pediatric / aspirin / Sulfa(Sulfonamide Antibiotics)\n\nChief Complaint:\nunexplained worsening transaminitis\n\nHistory of Present Illness:\nMs. ___ is a ___ yo woman with a h/o sickle cell trait and \nG6PD deficiency with a recent miscarriage who initially \npresented to ___ on ___ with n/v/diarrhea, found to have \nunexplained rising transaminitis and thus, transferred to ___ \nfor further care.  \nPatient reports that she initially started feeling not well last \n___. She was recently evaluate by ob-gyn for vaginal \nbleeding and was found to have had a spontaneous abortion \n(<12w). Ob-gyn visit on ___ showed serum hcg of <5. Her \nsymptoms most prominantly included generalized fatigue and \nmalaise, severe nausea leading to 7 episodes of vomiting on \n___, headache, abdominal pain, and watery diarrhea. Diarrhea \nand vomitus were both initially nonbloody.  \nOf note, patient reports some \"cold\" symptoms a few weeks prior \nin which she had a pharyngitis and took a lot of Nyquil. She was \nafraid she had an STD in her throat, but these symptoms did \nresolve. She does endorse some pharyngitis associated with these \nrecent symptoms as well, a bit of a cough, but nonproductive. \nShe denies any sick contacts. She did take some Tylenol, but \ndenies exceeding ___ pills a day. She denies taking any \nprescription medications in the few months prior to admission. \nShe occasionally took some B12 supplements.  \nShe presented with these symptoms to ___ on ___. On \nadmission there, she was febrile to 101.8 with a leukocytosis of \n11.2 (with significant atypical lymphocytosis) and tachycardic \nto 110. ALT/AST were in the 200s on presentation. She was \ninitially treated with ceftriaxone/flagyl for 2 days, diagnosed \nwith viral gastroenteritis, and evaluated by both GI and ID.  \nFor her workup, serologic studies significant for weakly \npositive monospot, negative hepatitis panel, and normal iron \nstudies, copper and ceruloplasmin levels. She had a RUQ u/s that \nshowed splenomegaly at 18cm without any gallstones, biliary, or \nliver abnormalities. She developed some \"trace hematemasis\" with \na worsening anemia and thus, had an EGD on ___ that showed \nduodenal ulcerations. ID recommending holding antibiotics as \ninitial infectious workup including blood cultures and influeza \ntesting were negative. Patient was treated for her nausea with \ncompazine and zofran.  \nAs her trasaminitis was worsening with a negative / pending \ninfectious work-up, she was transferred to ___ for further \ncare.  \nUpon arrival to ___, patient is feeling okay. She denies any \ncurrent pain, nausea, or diarrhea. She feels mostly very scared \nabout her liver function worsening, to the point of tears.  \nROS: per HPI, otherwise negative\n\nBrief Hospital Course:\nMs. ___ is a ___ woman with recent spontaneous abortion, \nsore throat, splenomegaly, and other symptoms who presented to \n___ and was found to have transminitis.  After an initial \nimprovement in transaminases, they worsened and she was \ntransfered for further evaluation, during which she was \ndiagnosed with CMV viremia. \n\n# Acute Liver Injury: Given her constellation of symptoms \nconsistent with a mono-like illness and positive CMV PCR at ___ \n___, as well as positive CMV IgM, CMV viremia is the most \nlikely cause of her liver injury.  At this point, Ig panel \npending, unclear whether reactivation vs new infection.  At ___ \n___ prior to transfer, HIV Ab/Ag was negative and VL pending. \n EBV IgG and IgM were positive, as well. Only known risk factor \nfor viremia is relative immunosuppression from high alcohol \nintake.  Immunoglobulin panel sent for autoimmune hepatitis, \nwhich was unremarkable. Her transminases, INR, and Bili were \ndowntrending on discharge. \n  \n# CMV: As above, acute vs. reactivation of chronic infection \nwith CMV IgM/G panel pending at discharge. Given timing of \nrecent spontaneous abortion, it is possible that was related to \nCMV (TORCH infection). Patient has other risk factors for \nspontaneous abortion, including sickle cell trait and alcohol \nabuse. \n\n# coagulopathy: Unlikely to be cirrhosis given age and negative \nwork up for causes of cirrhosis in young patients \n(ceruloplasmin, etc). She does not have sigmata of chronic liver \ndisease. Could be due to poor nutritional intake over past few \nweeks. INR improving after vitain K, Plt improving as well.  \n  \n# thrombocytopenia: most likely due to viral infection, \nsplenomegaly. \n\n# Anemia: Patient has history of sickle cell trait and h/h on \ntransfer is at baseline. Lower values at OSH most likely related \nto acute blood loss from vaginal bleeding and possibly from \nduodenal bleeding given ulcers seen on EGD. No signs of active \nbleeding after transfer. Iron studies at OSH were re-assuring \nfor lack of iron deficiency or overload.  \n\n# h/o alcohol abuse: drinking the equivalent of a pint of hard \nalcohol several times a week. Thiamine and folate \nsupplementation was started and social work was consulted. \n\n# G6PD - No reaction or new medications started that are likely \nto lead to hemolysis. \n\nTRANSITIONAL ISSUES\n- Patient counseled to avoid contact sports and other potential \ntrauma to abdomen given splenomegaly\n- Patient has a history of HBV infection that appears to have \nbeen cleared (HbSab and HbcAb positive). However, would consider \nHBV viral load to confirm no active replication \n- Acute liver injury likely from primary CMV infection given CMV \nIgM and PCR positive at ___. However, CMV IgG \npending on discharge, so could not rule out reactivation if IgG \npositive. Needs ___ IgG and viral load\n- If patient has reactivation, consider further immunodeficiency \nwork-up and need for treatment \n- Trend LFT's\n- Encourage alcohol cessation. Patient scheduled for therapy \nappointment at ___\n- Follow-up HIV viral load (pending at ___\n- cousin ___ ___  \n\n \n___ on Admission:\nNone\n\nDischarge Diagnosis:\nCMV viremia\nTransaminitis\nSplenomegaly",
